#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055525} :activity/id #uuid "ac444111-b6ae-51fc-95ff-0528d2e2c32c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048442 :topic/id #uuid "9fb9cfe0-2d6b-5be3-a023-bef57b6f3340" :topic/math-academy-id 43} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055525 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055999 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the integral of a power function multiplied by a constant, like $5x^2.$ How do we proceed?\n\nAccording to the **constant factor rule**, we can just take the constant factor out of the integral, like this:\n\n$$\n\\int 5 x^2\\, \\textrm{d}x = 5\\cdot \\int x^2 \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the integral using the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot \\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\cdot (\\frac{{x}^{2+1}}{2+1})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use this trick in general. Whenever the integrand is a function $f(x)$ multiplied by a constant $k,$ we can simplify as\n\n$$\n\\int k f(x)\\, \\textrm{d}x = k \\int f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** We can only take *constant factors* out of integrals. We *cannot* take any variables out of integrals." :tutorial/id #uuid "f293df60-4b16-57ab-88c1-d784d4b5e36c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 698 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ffdf7765-69ef-51c8-80a1-d1032e705129" :step/math-academy-id 1346 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055526}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055526 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054609 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054520 :question/id #uuid "578a205e-7879-5f8e-b7b5-a91a134ec5b3" :question/math-academy-id "e-369"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "add45606-39f6-5a61-9535-867d2950d183" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049481}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055099} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233180} {